{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1495162-the-australian-setting-material-problem","id":1495162,"title":"The Australian setting material problem","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Werewolf: The Forsaken"],"posts":[{"date":"2022-10-18T07:26:44-07:00","id":1495162,"title":"The Australian setting material problem","content":"So obviously this commentary is coming about 7 years after the material I'm gonna be commenting on was released, and the material in question was released a year and a bit before Dark Eras, so there's some alignment problems with the material in canon anyway. Probably this issue can be easily dismissed... but I think it'd be valuable if it weren't - particularly for the writers at Onyx Path.<br /> <br /> I just wanted to say that the WtF2e Australian material is... balls? I think that's the nicest way to put it. It's balls.<br /> <br /> So &quot;The Uratha Dreaming&quot; from the core is... overwriting a diverse set of beliefs of an already marginalised people with a less than flattering view of their beliefs and history. Yes, this is a fictional setting, but it didn't have to be done this way.<br /> <br /> By way of example:<br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The Dreaming was a nightmare. The Diharim birthed everything from their blood, bone, and flesh, but have you ever seen a birth? </div> </div> </div>It goes on like that for a bit.<br /> <br /> For those unaware, The Dreaming (or Dreamtime) is the term for the collective narratives of creation for Indigenous Australians - not a specific era or period of time per se, but a set of traditions about the land and the people's connection to it, which differs by region and tribes. WtF2e seems to be attempting to syncretise the Aranda tribe's beliefs with werewolf lore, which... makes sense, that's what gets done with all religions in WtF2e. The problem is that indigenous stories already include battles between Dreaming spirits. It already includes massive land-altering events. It didn't need to be painted as universally misconceived by those people and also &quot;the <i>real</i> truth is just super-gory and horrible&quot; (which is very much the continuous impression given), and then also have their beliefs nailed down as a done and in the past thing - which runs directly contrary to the practices of Australian native peoples.<br /> <br /> The thing which really makes it bad though is that Indigenous Australians represent the oldest continuous civilisation on the planet - 50,000 years of continuous habitation, uninterrupted by outsiders until the arrival of the British. Europe may have had grander construction, but the Romans were a baby civilisation compared to Indigenous Australians. Australia has holy sites older than the Sumerian city of Uruk (the mining company Rio Tinto blew up a 46,000 year old still in use holy site two years ago, which... you can imagine how devastating that is). And uh... given that of any set of cultures in the world, Indigenous Australian uratha probably would have the best claim to actual unfiltered knowledge of Pangaea... <b>European settlers taught them about the Firstborn apparently.</b> That is a thing that someone decided to make canon. Not sure whether that was intended to be a &quot;pure untouched native people despoiled by Europeans' conflicts&quot; narrative, or an &quot;ignorant natives being taught civilisation&quot; narrative, or some Frankenstein hybrid of the two... but <i>it's definitely a choice.</i><br /> <br /> Oh and I feel it needs to be said, songlines aren't the Indigenous internet? They're the paths that creator beings trod across the country, they are the physical manifestation of these native stories. It seems like someone just... saw the word and decided &quot;oh, cool, lines that people sing along, bet that makes uratha life convenient&quot;? It'd be a bit like saying that Mount Olympus was conceptual, so it exists inside all of us - no, Olympus is a real mountain, people have climbed it, it has a national park, you can go there it's just really hard to climb so obviously the gods live atop it. I mean, cool beans... Indigenous werewolves are better coordinated even without the Tribes... but... like, what? I guess in CofD terms songlines are basically leylines if we want to simplify it, but it's just a super weird choice to decide that Australia's big advantage was just having the phone before the phone was invented.<br /> <br /> On the flipside, it's cool that some awareness of native Dreamtime beings was put in there. Even just communicating the names is a big step for sharing <i>some</i> elements of native traditions. That's important.<br /> <br /> But... overall it feels like a very bad miss? Like, the writers, having encountered the existence of a people whose whole faith is that creation is a continuing thing, that they themselves are part of it through their stories and practices, and that those stories are both the history of the land and the shaping of the land which is still ongoing... nobody thought &quot;Hey this might be a good place to have the Temenos play a part of WtF2e&quot; instead? If the Dreamtime is the active participation of the Temenos in the material between the native residents of the land and the land itself, then those beliefs are <i>valid </i>and don't conflict with WtF setting lore. It'd make the Hisil in Australia a very different place as well because goetia would make up a big portion of the spirit courts, and that would be strange and confusing to outsiders. Sorry Business rites (burial and bereavement) also make this more complex, as ancestors may themselves be part of the Hisil - but potentially as goetia who are not to be named that are part of the land rather than ghosts. It could have been very different is what I'm saying, and it's not like there aren't places where the world works differently in CofD (looking at the Underworld issues in southern Africa for example).<br /> <br /> Because the real kick in the nards is, canonically the beliefs of these uratha (and by extension the native people they represent) are wrong. Pangaea didn't end because the Dreamtime spirits asked Luna and Wolf to make it end, but that is apparently what is believed in Australia in CofD. While the Dreamtime spirits may be canonically real... pretty much the entirety of everyone's beliefs about them isn't, and what little &quot;truth&quot; is remembered is an overwriting of those beliefs with what is itself also a misremembered horror story about Pangaea. Which kinda sucks.<br /> <br /> I feel like Onyx Path normally does better than that.<br /> <br /> Also, why you gotta make a whole major Australian city basically uninhabitable? Does someone have a grudge against Australia at OP? The Demon material for Australia's okay but in both Beast and Werewolf you kinda did Australia dirty.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\">The Yozis are right (and you should suffer and obey for all eternity).</span></b>","edited":{"userid":1487,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1487-zahaqiel","user":"Zahaqiel","date":". 10-18-2022, 10:55 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1487-zahaqiel","name":"Zahaqiel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1487_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"41"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-18T12:12:27-07:00","id":1495176,"title":"","content":"The Australian settings were provided by the late, great <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/leath-sheales-has-passed-away/\" target=\"_blank\">Leath Sheales</a>, who was a native Australian himself. You're welcome to go scrawling through his posts here to try and find answers for accountability (his username was Bunyip).<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> It'd be a bit like saying that Mount Olympus was conceptual, so it exists inside all of us - no, Olympus is a real mountain, people have climbed it, it has a national park, you can go there it's just really hard to climb so obviously the gods live atop it. </div> </div> </div>I don't have enough interest in this conversation to address the rest of this, but considering that this is a setting that understood, like in real life, the Ancient Greeks knew there were not gods at the top of the actual Olympus, but also has a literal soul space of humanity where a conceptual Olympus exists where the conceptual gods exist, I find saying &quot;Mount Olympus was conceptual, so it exists inside all of us&quot; with an implied eye-roll hilarious. I mean, that's actually, literally true in the Chronicles world.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":99,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","user":"ArcaneArts","date":". 10-18-2022, 06:01 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-18T15:01:37-07:00","id":1495194,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1495162-the-australian-setting-material-problem?p=1495176#post1495176\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Australian settings were provided by the late, great <a href=\"https://&amp;quot;&#91;url\" target=\"_blank\">http://theonyxpath.com/leath-sheales-has-passed-away/&amp;quot;]Leath</a> Sheales[/URL], who was a native Australian himself. You're welcome to go scrawling through his posts here to try and find answers for accountability (his username was Bunyip).<br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I don't have enough interest in this conversation to address the rest of this, but considering that this is a setting that understood, like in real life, the Ancient Greeks knew there were not gods at the top of the actual Olympus, but also has a literal soul space where a conceptual Olympus exists where the conceptual gods exist, I find saying &amp;quot;Mount Olympus was conceptual, so it exists inside all of us&amp;quot; with an implied eye-roll hilarious. I mean, that's actually, literally true in the Chronicles world.</div> </div> </div> </div>Ahh man. I had no idea. May he rest in peace and may his family do well knowing his positive impact in the world","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/24173-primordial-newcomer","name":"Primordial newcomer","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1965"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-18T17:47:57-07:00","id":1495207,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zahaqiel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1495162-the-australian-setting-material-problem#post1495162\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Like, the writers, having encountered the existence of a people whose whole faith is that creation is a continuing thing, that they themselves are part of it through their stories and practices, and that those stories are both the history of the land and the shaping of the land which is still ongoing... nobody thought &quot;Hey this might be a good place to have the Temenos play a part of WtF2e&quot; instead? If the Dreamtime is the active participation of the Temenos in the material between the native residents of the land and the land itself, then those beliefs are <i>valid </i>and don't conflict with WtF setting lore. It'd make the Hisil in Australia a very different place as well because goetia would make up a big portion of the spirit courts, and that would be strange and confusing to outsiders. Sorry Business rites (burial and bereavement) also make this more complex, as ancestors may themselves be part of the Hisil - but potentially as goetia who are not to be named that are part of the land rather than ghosts. It could have been very different is what I'm saying, and it's not like there aren't places where the world works differently in CofD (looking at the Underworld issues in southern Africa for example).</div> </div> </div> </div>I want you to think for a moment about what you're seemingly pushing for in this paragraph, because it is by no means a small thing.<br /> <br /> You are proposing that a sample setting in the core rulebook \u2014 already not working with a surfeit of wordcount and pagespace \u2014 should integrate a part of the wider cosmology that Werewolf does not typically deal with on account of its distance from practically-engaged-with reality in general and werewolves' lack of direct participation in it in particular, integrating beings that normally don't leave the interior of the human soul into a dimension whose closest Astral reflection is further afield than human cultural icons deal with, and to justify this you're pointing to a setting that gets an entire chapter to itself as part of the historical setting books that are one to two times the size of a corebook and get easily ten times the corebook sample settings' three-to-four pages to work with.<br /> <br /> Let's set aside for a moment that the slice of the Astral that Chronicles has most consistently identified with Australian Aboriginal cultural practices is the Dreaming Earth, which already communicates in the ecstatic language of pain and hunger more familiar to the Shadow, down to werewolves interacting with it differently than most other supernatural beings, whereas the goetia that come from people aren't privy to the interiority of the Uratha and mostly exert influence on people instead of the wider world due to there being a fairly large barrier between those parts of the world-soul: There is no good reason to try and jam the Astral into a four-page sample setting in the corebook for a game whose protagonists will never normally interact with the Astral and there's already a chronicle-naming out-of-context problem that would have to mess with it.<br /> <br /> &quot;This story is true&quot; is a recurring line in Werewolf because it's the same framing device urban legends use but with a more generalized gravitas. It's been explicit sing at least Tribes of the Moon that werewolves are not attempting to convey factual information when they begin a story with &quot;This story is true,&quot; and that explicit statement comes in a section of the Iron Masters chapter talking about stories changing to suit the times as part of the tribe's greater emphasis on adaptability. Yes, the events described in the Uratha Dreaming account don't match up with those described in The Sundered World; that Dark Era also directly calls attention to multiple points of modern werewolf belief that are factually untrue. Werewolves get shit wrong with some frequency when it comes to ancient history and more esoteric metaphysics, because they are creatures of the present with a job to do. My (admittedly extremely loose) understanding of how Australian Aboriginal oral history manages to keep narrative drift from happening and maintain the fidelity of the old stories involves a degree of fact-checking that you can't really manage when you're the only werewolf in a tribe full of humans and all of your peers are working from information in a different part of the landmass.<br /> <br /> The text never compares songlines to the internet \u2014 the words &quot;songline&quot; and &quot;network&quot; being used in the same sentence does not imply that, and it's kind of ridiculous to suppose that, in a paragraph talking about werewolves from across the continent keeping in touch in a context where most human tribes have one werewolf each, a lodge being born from these interactions and using spirit-magic to keep in contact must mean a set of geographical features with cultural significance to specific groups of people have been misinterpreted as an abstract concept.<br /> <br /> By the opposite token, the Firstborn aren't abstract beings with evenly-distributed physical/ephemeral presence (Basra is right there in this chapter to counterpoint that assumption), nor is their lack of patronage to Australian werewolves much of an implication of anything given that the tribes were formed at the behest of werewolves and continue to gain new members through the cultural activities of those same werewolves. You're welcome to read what you like into the stated narrative of &quot;the Dreaming Lodge encountered tribal werewolves when they arrived on the continent, then spread the knowledge of how to join up through their network,&quot; but given the age of the Pure-Forsaken conflict and werewolf society's general remove from human cultural movement I feel as though it's unreasonable to leap to either of your assumed readings instead of &quot;this was an existing set of ideologies and conflict that happened to be brought to Australia through the same vector that brought outside werewolves to Australia with outside humans.&quot;<br /> <br /> &quot;Unfiltered knowledge of Pangaea,&quot; if we make the not-guaranteed assumption that the things of The Sundered World introduced to the playerbase were known by the writers at the time the Idigam Chronicle was being written, amounts to &quot;the space between worlds is extremely trippy, contains hybrids of flesh and spirit, and is ruled over by a bunch of things that can no longer exist in their original state.&quot; That's not useful information to most werewolves, particularly in a culture where the tribes focusing on spirits and Ridden are in the minority in the Lodge due to differences in priorities.<br /> <br /> So yeah, this appears to be a combination of werewolf syncretism and you reading more into the text than is there.<br /> <br /> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\"><div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Also, why you gotta make a whole major Australian city basically uninhabitable? Does someone have a grudge against Australia at OP? The Demon material for Australia's okay but in both Beast and Werewolf you kinda did Australia dirty. </div> </div> </div>&quot;Something haunts a major Australian city in a way that is routinely preventing any lasting supernatural presence besides the Bunyip from taking root in it&quot; is a story hook in Beast's sample settings in the same way as &quot;Makara in Vancouver have access to anti-Kinship that shuts down supernatural powers&quot; or &quot;there's a serial killer stalking the Primordial Dream for Begotten souls in Orkney.&quot; You might as well ask if I have a grudge against Chicago for putting demons in it in my Changeling game, never mind that there's a world of difference between &quot;here's a mystery for a Beast game&quot; and &quot;it is universal canon in the Chronicles of Darkness that there is one monster in Adelaide&quot; \u2014 you're allowed to not use the sample settings as-written.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-18T19:08:51-07:00","id":1495214,"title":"","content":"I'm gonna preface this post with a little reminder that nobody is being attacked here. I am not calling anyone bad, demanding a complete rewrite or otherwise trying to undermine what people enjoy about WtF2e. I am commenting on inadequate representation, and suggesting alternative ways that things could have been handled. There's some snark in this post, but that also is not intended as an attack but rather an attempt to highlight bad takes and behaviours that we gamers often fall into that are anti-social and counter-productive. The reason we have these kinds of conversations is to improve the quality of the material going forward, and expand the way people think about creative work so that it better serves people who want to play it.<br /> <br /> With that being said...<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>ArcaneArts</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1495162-the-australian-setting-material-problem?p=1495176#post1495176\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The Australian settings were provided by the late, great <a href=\"http://theonyxpath.com/leath-sheales-has-passed-away/\" target=\"_blank\">Leath Sheales</a>, who was a native Australian himself. You're welcome to go scrawling through his posts here to try and find answers for accountability (his username was Bunyip).</div> </div> </div> </div> I'll do that. Thanks. This is something that has ticked off a lot of my friends and made them not want to play Werewolf so much (or at least, completely ignore everything OP writes about Australia), so that is helpful.<br /> <br /> To be clear though when you say &quot;native Australian&quot;... is that like &quot;native New Yorker&quot; or...?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I don't have enough interest in this conversation to address the rest of this, but considering that this is a setting that understood, like in real life, the Ancient Greeks knew there were not gods at the top of the actual Olympus, but also has a literal soul space of humanity where a conceptual Olympus exists where the conceptual gods exist, I find saying &quot;Mount Olympus was conceptual, so it exists inside all of us&quot; with an implied eye-roll hilarious. I mean, that's actually, literally true in the Chronicles world. </div> </div> </div>Sure. But Mount Olympus is an actual real place you can walk to too, which is what the songlines <i>are</i>. The way it's pitched in the book is... the conceptual without the physical. Or at least close enough to to give that impression to people who don't know better (much like Hollywood has kinda taken that Mount Olympus is a place that doesn't real - hence why I used it as an example, Olympus is not the same as Meru but for some reason a lot of people think of it like Meru, as entirely mythical rather than real with mythical associations).<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1495162-the-australian-setting-material-problem?p=1495207#post1495207\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I want you to think for a moment about what you're seemingly pushing for in this paragraph, because it is by no means a small thing.<br /> <br /> You are proposing that a sample setting in the core rulebook \u2014 already not working with a surfeit of wordcount and pagespace \u2014 should integrate a part of the wider cosmology that Werewolf does not typically deal with on account of its distance from practically-engaged-with reality in general and werewolves' lack of direct participation in it in particular, integrating beings that normally don't leave the interior of the human soul into a dimension whose closest Astral reflection is further afield than human cultural icons deal with, and to justify this you're pointing to a setting that gets an entire chapter to itself as part of the historical setting books that are one to two times the size of a corebook and get easily ten times the corebook sample settings' three-to-four pages to work with.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm actually not pushing for that, but it's super cool that you automatically assumed a request for better recognition and treatment was actually &quot;please rewrite the whole book to be about this one thing&quot;. Shows a real attempt to listen to what is actually being said.<br /> <br /> OP writers put a lot of vaguely defined fluff material that probably could use some mechanics to support them in their books all the time. Heck the number of lodges in WtF2e that could use an actual mechanical writeup is well into the 20s or 30s. There's even one in the very section we're talking about, with its own lodge rite for which there are no mechanics. So no, a section outlining a place that is different doesn't <i>need</i> a full writeup to be a section outlining a place that is different. To bring it back to another example location, Dark Eras gets away with having the entire Underworld be broken across half a continent and there are no real mechanical explanations as to what that means for the various splats that exist across that continent, and that's fine. STs get to make things up for their own games all the time, everyone gets that, and it's disingenuous to make this line of argument as an objection to better representation.<br /> <br /> But inversely, your line of reasoning is also kinda not valid even on its own terms either? It's the same line of reasoning someone could have used during the end of 1e to say that it's unreasonable to demand that the new version of Requiem get a whole chapter dedicated to a single one-shot monster from a late period supplement, and yet... Strix Chronicles. At no point have I asked for a Strix Chronicles-esque treatment for Australia (although it'd be nice, it's also definitely unreasonable to monopolise the core like that for only one continent's material). WtF2e could have been a lot of things and after-the-fact saying that it could only have been the way it was is... not a great argument.<br /> <br /> And CofD, while written for a mainline mode in each splat, also caters to the wider shared universe as well, so even a mild hat-tip in that direction would have opened doors for STs. Some of that work has since been done with Dark Eras and then later the much more detailed level of that work with Contagion Chronicles but Contagion Chronicles isn't great for mixing with this particular cultural issue either, and we kinda got skipped in Dark Eras because apparently we're too boring historically.<br /> <br /> Maybe, in future, you might want to consider phrasing this kind of objection with &quot;I don't think there'd be the word count to do that idea justice&quot; rather than some variation on &quot;BUT HAVE YOU THOUGHT THROUGH WHAT THIS WOULD MEAN FOR MAH BOOKS?!&quot;? I dunno, just seems like it might be a better way not to drive people off from talking to you.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Let's set aside for a moment that the slice of the Astral that Chronicles has most consistently identified with Australian Aboriginal cultural practices is the Dreaming Earth, which already communicates in the ecstatic language of pain and hunger more familiar to the Shadow, down to werewolves interacting with it differently than most other supernatural beings, whereas the goetia that come from people aren't privy to the interiority of the Uratha and mostly exert influence on people instead of the wider world due to there being a fairly large barrier between those parts of the world-soul </div> </div> </div>Let's, as most of those conclusions don't appear in any way to be canon in 2e. See also: everything related to the Astral in Mage and Beast, and the distinct absence of much of anything on the Dreaming Earth.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> There is no good reason to try and jam the Astral into a four-page sample setting in the corebook for a game whose protagonists will never normally interact with the Astral and there's already a chronicle-naming out-of-context problem that would have to mess with it. </div> </div> </div>Can't have Idigam and the Astral coinciding in the same setting material! No, that'd be... *checks notes* ...an opening to a new potential aspect of Idigam horror! I mean it's not like WtF2e contains explicit references to other material in it like... *checks notes* ...oh wait no, it has an explicit sidebar reference to Inferno and lets STs work out how to put that material in the game on their own too.<br /> <br /> You see what I mean about how this stuff isn't that hard?<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;This story is true&quot; is a recurring line in Werewolf because it's the same framing device urban legends use but with a more generalized gravitas. </div> </div> </div>Mmm... and condescension is when you talk down to people.<br /> <br /> My objection isn't to the use of &quot;This story is true&quot;. My objection is to the fact that the <i>sole representation of native stories</i> is that the Dreamtime (now dead and gone, not current and alive) was an awful period of time and bad for everyone. It's kind of important that you grasp that difference, otherwise you're going to be going off at some imaginary alt-universe post I didn't make.<br /> <br /> See, in Requiem, the OP writers never decided to make the Lancea have the true secret story of Jesus who needed to murder babies to power his miracles and who occasionally exploded bodily out of other people as a means of quick teleportation, because it might be... I dunno... disrespectful to say that the entire Christian world was wrong about a central figure of their faith, and that instead he was a gore-fest nightmare that it's best people don't remember accurately. Instead they picked an ancillary character from the gospels who could unobtrusively be given an alternate history <i>without</i> making out that an entire faith is fundamentally wrong. Even in WtF2e, when they've engaged in syncretist adaptation of other faiths, they've kinda done a better job of &quot;This is werewolves attempting to use one to explain the other&quot; rather than &quot;Hey cub, you're wrong about what you believe&quot;.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Yes, the events described in the Uratha Dreaming account don't match up with those described in The Sundered World; that Dark Era also directly calls attention to multiple points of modern werewolf belief that are factually untrue. Werewolves get shit wrong with some frequency when it comes to ancient history and more esoteric metaphysics, because they are creatures of the present with a job to do. My (admittedly extremely loose) understanding of how Australian Aboriginal oral history manages to keep narrative drift from happening and maintain the fidelity of the old stories involves a degree of fact-checking that you can't really manage when you're the only werewolf in a tribe full of humans and all of your peers are working from information in a different part of the landmass. </div> </div> </div>Mmm, I even explicitly stated that I recognised that WtF2e came out a year and a half before The Sundered World, and that there were going to be misalignments between canon. Almost like I wanted to communicate that I had an appreciation for that issue being at play or something.<br /> <br /> The thing is, every other werewolf culture on the planet in WtF2e is a baby culture by comparison. The werewolves of Australia are part of a culture that has literally maintained sites for 50,000 years and the accompanying lore and stories and practices. It's a straight line from Sundered World to today. And sure, every uratha faction has some untrue things in what they believe. This section kinda pitches that it's all untrue? Except the gore which mostly replaces native stories? It's not great.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> The text never compares songlines to the internet \u2014 the words &quot;songline&quot; and &quot;network&quot; being used in the same sentence does not imply that, and it's kind of ridiculous to suppose that, in a paragraph talking about werewolves from across the continent keeping in touch in a context where most human tribes have one werewolf each, a lodge being born from these interactions and using spirit-magic to keep in contact must mean a set of geographical features with cultural significance to specific groups of people have been misinterpreted as an abstract concept. </div> </div> </div>No, I made that comparison for hyperbole. That's this rhetorical technique where you overstate something to make a point.<br /> <br /> One thing though... every other continent already had messenger spirits and the Messenger rite?<br /> <br /> So yeah I don't know what exactly they were trying to give the Dreaming Lodge with the whole songline thing, it's just perhaps not exactly properly thought through (and as I stated above, it kinda gives people the wrong impression of what songlines are in a Hollywood-movie/Olympus sort of way).<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> By the opposite token, the Firstborn aren't abstract beings with evenly-distributed physical/ephemeral presence (Basra is right there in this chapter to counterpoint that assumption), nor is their lack of patronage to Australian werewolves much of an implication of anything given that the tribes were formed at the behest of werewolves and continue to gain new members through the cultural activities of those same werewolves. You're welcome to read what you like into the stated narrative of &quot;the Dreaming Lodge encountered tribal werewolves when they arrived on the continent, then spread the knowledge of how to join up through their network,&quot; but given the age of the Pure-Forsaken conflict and werewolf society's general remove from human cultural movement I feel as though it's unreasonable to leap to either of your assumed readings instead of &quot;this was an existing set of ideologies and conflict that happened to be brought to Australia through the same vector that brought outside werewolves to Australia with outside humans.&quot; </div> </div> </div>Sure, but that last thing you said is one of the possible narrative lines I explicitly suggested they might have been going for <i>that wasn't a good one</i> (you know, the whole &quot;pure untouched natives being influenced by the conflicts of Europeans&quot; thing). It's not a great way to paint native peoples, and Onyx Path have been pretty good at recognising that in the case of native Americans. Not so much here though.<br /> <br /> It's worth mentioning for additional context that Indigenous Australians were treated fairly similarly by British colonists to the way that South Americans were treated by the Spanish a few centuries prior. Part of the reason Australia was settled instead of New Zealand when NZ has so many more natural resources and a distinct lack of predator animals is that the Maori drove them off (eventually leading to the British treating them like a full sovereign nation), but the native tribes of Australia were pretty passive and (to hear the British tell it) could make no claims to any territories in Australia, making it supposedly easy pickings for the British to move in and claim everything. Their presence also had an immediate negative affect on the health of native peoples (with the unintentional infliction of measles, smallpox and tuberculosis epidemics), and in subsequent decades they also engaged in multiple extermination attempts against them (look up &quot;the Black War&quot; on wikipedia if you want to read about just one instance of the genocide as an example). Seems a bit weird for it then to be canon that the native people just straight up immediately adopted supposedly European Tribe ideas upon first contact when the people that were doing the talking were obviously bringing so many bad things - particularly when uratha can <i>see the spirits that people bring with them</i> and so would have had the earliest warnings of anyone about what was to follow.<br /> <br /> But also, let's unpack (no pun intended) what you're saying here. Sure, the Firstborn are singular beings, not world-spanning concepts. They were also Father Wolf's own pack. So the one culture that has a straight shot from Sundered World to today... knew about Urfarah... but not his pack... and none of his pack had any interactions with anyone from this continent or any of their ancestors... and that sounds plausible to you? It seems improbable to me. I could see them having different takes on the Tribes, or perhaps even their own Firstborn... but it's super weird to be entirely ignorant of the Firstborn until the late 1700s/early 1800s.<br /> <br /> Maybe Firstborn just can't swim? For 50,000 years? And the continent that is commonly known to be host to some of the most deadly creatures on the planet, and whose territorial waters are home to the deadliest sharks probably has nothing they'd want to hunt in its Hisil either? Yeah, that makes sense. Heck, our marsupial megafauna all got hunted to extinction <i>by us humans</i> 25,000 years ago, so that's definitely not something that the Firstborn would have been drawn to. That's probably it. Australia's probably just been too boring to have any Firstborn visit. For the entire history of its inhabitation, including the time of Pangaea. That's gotta be it.<br /> <br /> Should I give you an incredulous stare now, or do I need to belabour that point further? &quot;Victorian werewolves teach hunter-gatherers ancient werewolf hunting lore&quot; should be an immediate red flag even before you factor in which continent we're talking about, but it does pay to remember which continent we're talking about.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> &quot;Unfiltered knowledge of Pangaea,&quot; if we make the not-guaranteed assumption that the things of The Sundered World introduced to the playerbase were known by the writers at the time the Idigam Chronicle was being written, amounts to &quot;the space between worlds is extremely trippy, contains hybrids of flesh and spirit, and is ruled over by a bunch of things that can no longer exist in their original state.&quot; That's not useful information to most werewolves, particularly in a culture where the tribes focusing on spirits and Ridden are in the minority in the Lodge due to differences in priorities. </div> </div> </div>See previous regarding both the misalignment of these books, and also Urfarah's pack. You seem to have skipped over the part where the Firstborn are part of Sundered World lore.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <span style=\"font-family:inherit\">&quot;Something haunts a major Australian city in a way that is routinely preventing any lasting supernatural presence besides the Bunyip from taking root in it&quot; is a story hook in Beast's sample settings in the same way as &quot;Makara in Vancouver have access to anti-Kinship that shuts down supernatural powers&quot; or &quot;there's a serial killer stalking the Primordial Dream for Begotten souls in Orkney.&quot; You might as well ask if I have a grudge against Chicago for putting demons in it in my Changeling game, never mind that there's a world of difference between &quot;here's a mystery for a Beast game&quot; and &quot;it is universal canon in the Chronicles of Darkness that there is one monster in Adelaide&quot; \u2014 you're allowed to not use the sample settings as-written.</span> </div> </div> </div>Sure, but I cited it as part of a pattern. I get that Australia is the death continent and all, but like... the only vaguely positive setting material we get is about Deva Corp facilities in the Blue Mountains.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\">The Yozis are right (and you should suffer and obey for all eternity).</span></b>","edited":{"userid":1487,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1487-zahaqiel","user":"Zahaqiel","date":". 10-19-2022, 12:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1487-zahaqiel","name":"Zahaqiel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1487_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"41"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-18T22:05:45-07:00","id":1495219,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Zahaqiel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/werewolf-the-forsaken/1495162-the-australian-setting-material-problem?p=1495214#post1495214\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To be clear though when you say &quot;native Australian&quot;... is that like &quot;native New Yorker&quot; or...?<br /> .</div> </div> </div> </div>I do not have a polite way to address this response to a beloved member of our community, a talented and inventive writer, and a personal friend who is now dead.<br /> <br /> Well, except <i>maybe think before you speak</i>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Kelly R.S. Steele, Freelance Writer(Feel free to call me Kelly, Arcane, or Arc)</b><br /> The world is not beautiful, therefore it is.-Keiichi Sigsawa, Kino's Journey<br /> Feminine pronouns, please.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/99-arcanearts","name":"ArcaneArts","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar99_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11384"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-18T22:26:44-07:00","id":1495221,"title":"","content":"Sorry that was poorly phrased - I'm not attempting to cast aspersions or be disrespectful. I'm attempting to understand where he was coming from, and what you meant by saying what you said, and even just the right way to refer to him under the circumstances.<br /> <br /> I didn't know him, I don't know what traditions he followed or the appropriate way to refer to him following his death. If he was Ngunnawal or any other tribe, then I want to respect that. &quot;Native Australian&quot; is a term that can mean multiple things and easily implies things that may not be correct.<br /> <br /> Part of the whole thing I'm trying to raise to people's awareness here is that default assumptions are often wrong when dealing with other cultures - in Australia &quot;native Australian&quot; does not mean the same thing that &quot;Native American&quot; means in the US, it's not synonymous with Indigenous people it simply means someone was born here. Someone can be native and non-Indigenous, and someone can be Indigenous and non-native (there's even been recent federal-level legal battles about citizenship rights in exactly that scenario, because of course there has, life is awful). Appropriate tradition when someone is Indigenous and deceased is to not use their name or depictions of their likeness, and that is what I was concerned about and the main reason why I asked.<br /> <br /> I checked everywhere I could before asking, I could find zero answers. I probably should have explained it when I asked because obviously... the whole point here is we often miss cultural issues in cultures that aren't our own... so it's super dumb of me to fail to properly communicate this one.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\">The Yozis are right (and you should suffer and obey for all eternity).</span></b>","edited":{"userid":1487,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1487-zahaqiel","user":"Zahaqiel","date":".  Realised I hadn't adequately addressed the cultural disconnect 10-19-2022, 03:42 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1487-zahaqiel","name":"Zahaqiel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1487_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"41"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-19T05:56:38-07:00","id":1495237,"title":"","content":"Yeah, having gone through all 35 pages of his posts on the forum, it appears he didn't discuss this element of Forsaken lore as far as I can find. And since he didn't get into it, that's obviously that for figuring out what specifically he was aiming for. Speculation would be really crappy and non-productive, and it kinda matters more what is actually in the text and what it does or does not indicate to people.<br /> <br /> There's just a lot of cultural knowledge that doesn't really cross large distances from their source very well is the real problem.<br /> <br /> Things like songlines <i>aren't</i> just locally meaningful geographic features like Satchel put forward, they're not singular local holy sites, they're the actual paths between places that the song both describes and describes the historical meaning of. You can physically navigate by songline, it's both story and guide. And they're still being made - there's post-colonial songlines related to the stories of contact, related to missionaries who visited certain peoples, etc as well. So that's why it's weird for someone to use one as a spirit-magic phone line... because the song is the path itself and a singular message about the path that pays respect to it as you walk it. You can't really use a songline without <i>walking the songline</i>, and then you're also singing the song of that line, so... those two things go hand in hand.<br /> <br /> It could make some sense to mind-walk a songline, but then that gets back to that whole Astral/werewolves problem that apparently needs a whole book rewrite. :P","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\">The Yozis are right (and you should suffer and obey for all eternity).</span></b>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1487-zahaqiel","name":"Zahaqiel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1487_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"41"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2022-10-19T14:49:09-07:00","id":1495272,"title":"","content":"<span style=\"color:#FF0000\"><b>Yeah, this thread has gotten nasty in several respects and lots of complaints so I'm going to be shutting it down.</b></span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Author of <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Cthulhu-Armageddon-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01KUOM7SI/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982844&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Cthulhu+Armageddon\" target=\"_blank\">Cthulhu Armageddon</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Teenage-Weredeer-Bright-Falls-Mysteries-ebook/dp/B075TTN2FN/ref=sr_1_2?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982717&amp;sr=1-2&amp;keywords=Weredeer#customerReviews\" target=\"_blank\">I was a Teenage Weredeer</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Straight-Outta-Fangton-Comedic-Vampire-ebook/dp/B01KNHICCC/ref=sr_1_1?s=digital-text&amp;ie=UTF8&amp;qid=1535982774&amp;sr=1-1&amp;keywords=Straight+Outta+Fangton\" target=\"_blank\">Straight Outta Fangton</a></i>, <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Lucifers-Star-C-T-Phipps-ebook/dp/B01MDM9MCT/ref=mt_kindle?_encoding=UTF8&amp;me=&amp;qid=\" target=\"_blank\">Lucifer's Star</a>, </i>and the <i><a href=\"https://www.amazon.com/Rules-Supervillainy-Saga-Book-ebook/dp/B07MB89S33/\" target=\"_blank\">Supervillainy Saga</a>.</i><br /> <br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12002-ctphipps","name":"CTPhipps","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12002_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"13933"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}